  "headline_index": 0,
  "what_the_study_did": "The study retrospectively analyzed and ranked commonly used murine models for metabolic dysfunction-associated steatotic liver disease (MASLD) based on their similarity to human MASLD.",
  "what_it_found": "Western diets (WD) and choline-deficient diets (CDD) showed varying degrees of similarity to human MASLD, with WD models better mirroring metabolic aspects and CDD models better featuring pro-fibrotic components.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "The lack of a standard translationally relevant preclinical model has hindered the field’s ability to study the chronic and complex pathophysiology of MASH.",
      "topic": "Model limitations"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Disappointingly, within each diet macro-category, relatively subtle differences in micronutrient and macronutrient composition (for example, amount of cholesterol or choline) and study designs are sufficient to introduce variability in MASLD disease endpoints.",
      "topic": "Diet variability"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Our approach identified murine MASLD models showing (phenotypic and/or histologic and/or transcriptomic) profiles relevant to human MASH, which are suitable for most preclinical experimental applications.",
      "topic": "Model identification"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Murine MASLD transcriptomes are close to humans but do not predict fibrosis efficiently.",
      "topic": "Transcriptome limitations"
    },
    {
      "quote": "This analysis indicates that despite variations in specific single gene expression, the biological pathways implicated in the disease were consistently regulated across both the animal models and human cases.",
      "topic": "Pathway consistency"
    },
    {
      "quote": "Our study provides several important outcomes and unique insights. A key strength of our study lies in the uniformity of methodology applied across all models for staining and scoring liver histopathology.",
